# Hand-written minimal Matter node. Distinct from the Nordic/CHIP Matter stack:
# this is the from-scratch implementation, and it exists because CHIP does not
# fit a 512 KB / 128 KB part.

config TINYMATTER
	bool "Hand-written minimal Matter node"
	help
	  Compile the from-scratch Matter protocol code: TLV codec, message and
	  exchange layers, MRP, and the small cluster surface Apple Home
	  actually exercises.

	  This is NOT a certified Matter stack and does not aim to be. It exists
	  so a DWM3001CDK can receive its credential reader identity from Apple Home
	  over SetAliroReaderConfig, which is the only way this board can be
	  provisioned without cloning a credential from a donor.

	  Off by default; every stage lands behind it before anything depends
	  on it.

config TINYMATTER_MULTI_ADMIN
	bool "Multi-administrator commissioning: a second ecosystem on this lock"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default n
	help
	  Hold up to five fabrics instead of three, keep access control per
	  fabric rather than per node, and enforce it: without this the node
	  records a controller's ACL and consults it for nothing, which is
	  survivable while Apple Home is the only administrator and is not once
	  a second one exists.

	  This is what lets Home Assistant be added alongside Apple Home, and
	  therefore what lets anything write a binding.

	  Off, the node is the single-administrator server it has always been
	  and the two spare fabric slots and their access-control storage are
	  not compiled in.

config TINYMATTER_CLIENT
	bool "Matter client: start CASE and send commands to another node"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	# Not a taste dependency: a binding is written by an administrator, and
	# Apple Home does not write bindings. Without a second administrator to
	# do it, a client build is configurable by nobody.
	select TINYMATTER_MULTI_ADMIN
	# Finding the bound peer is a DNS-SD service query, and OpenThread's DNS
	# client is not in a default build: MEASURED at 4,052 B of flash and 160 B
	# of RAM on this image, which is why it arrives with the feature that
	# needs it rather than being on for everyone. Conditional, because this
	# module also builds on hosts and ports that have no OpenThread at all.
	#
	# Guarded on OPENTHREAD and NOT on NET_L2_OPENTHREAD, which is the one
	# that looks right and is not: this app drives OpenThread's own API
	# directly and never brings up Zephyr's L2, so that symbol is unset and a
	# select behind it silently does nothing. MEASURED: the resulting image
	# had CONFIG_TINYMATTER_CLIENT=y and
	# `# CONFIG_OPENTHREAD_DNS_CLIENT is not set`, and still linked, because
	# nothing called the resolver yet and LTO dropped it. It would have
	# failed as an undefined otDnsClientResolveService the moment something
	# did.
	select OPENTHREAD_DNS_CLIENT if OPENTHREAD
	default n
	help
	  Compile the CASE INITIATOR: the half of the handshake this node needs
	  before it can send a command to another node on the fabric, rather
	  than only answer one.

	  This is what lets the lock unlock a SECOND lock directly when the UWB
	  gate fires -- the Matter binding pattern, with no hub automation in
	  the path. The node stays a full Door Lock server either way; the
	  client role is additive.

	  Off, not one byte of it is compiled: the sources live in their own
	  file, guarded in the CMakeLists rather than inside the code.

config TINYMATTER_DOOR_LOCK_ALARMS
	bool "Door Lock DoorLockAlarm event"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default n
	help
	  Compile the DoorLockAlarm event into the Door Lock cluster by
	  defining MATTER_FEATURE_DL_ALARMS=1 for the module and its callers.

	  Turn it on only for a lock with a sensor that can witness an alarm.
	  Off, every source file preprocesses to what it was before alarms
	  existed, so the default image stays byte-identical.

config TINYMATTER_CRYPTO_PSA
	bool "P-256 and AES-ECB on PSA Crypto (backends/psa)"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default y
	help
	  Provide the five P-256 seams (DAC signing, operational key
	  generation, CASE ECDH, sign and verify) and crypto_aes_ecb_encrypt()
	  on PSA Crypto. psa_crypto_init() must run before the node starts.
	  Turn it off only to supply those six functions from elsewhere.

choice TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P
	prompt "SPAKE2+ P-256 backend"
	depends on TINYMATTER
	default TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_OBERON if NRF_SECURITY
	default TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_MBEDTLS

config TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_OBERON
	bool "nrf_oberon (nRF Connect SDK, Nordic parts only)"
	help
	  Link the four ocrypto_spake2p_p256_* primitives from nrf_oberon,
	  which nrf_security already links when its PSA driver is the oberon
	  one. No code; see backends/oberon/oberon.cmake.

config TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_MBEDTLS
	bool "Mbed TLS 3.6 ECP (backends/mbedtls)"
	help
	  The same four primitives on Mbed TLS ECP, for any part. Needs
	  MBEDTLS_ECP_C, MBEDTLS_ECP_DP_SECP256R1_ENABLED and
	  MBEDTLS_PSA_CRYPTO_C in the Mbed TLS configuration.

endchoice

# ---- the Zephyr port (ports/zephyr) ------------------------------------------
# Carried from the source project's application Kconfig, where these lived
# beside the app that used them.

config TINYMATTER_BLE
	bool "Matter commissioning transport: the 0xFFF6 GATT service over BTP"
	# The framing itself is in the core (src/) and has no Zephyr
	# dependency; this brings in the Zephyr-side adapter that carries it.
	depends on BT_PERIPHERAL
	select TINYMATTER
	default n
	help
	  Compile the 0xFFF6 service (C1 write, C2 indicate) and the BTP glue
	  that turns it into a Matter message transport.

	  This does not start advertising. The application owns the single
	  advertising set and asks this transport for the commissionable payload
	  while the node has no fabric -- see TINYMATTER_DISCRIMINATOR.

if TINYMATTER_BLE

config TINYMATTER_DISCRIMINATOR
	hex "Matter setup discriminator, 12-bit"
	default 0xF00
	range 0x000 0xFFF
	help
	  Goes out in the commissionable advertisement, and a commissioner uses
	  it to pick this device out of everything else advertising 0xFFF6. It
	  must match the discriminator encoded in the setup code the user enters
	  or the phone will never connect.

	  0xF00 with passcode 20202021 is the pairing CHIP's own test harnesses
	  use, which makes a bench commissioner work with no extra setup.

config TINYMATTER_VENDOR_ID
	hex "Matter vendor ID"
	default 0xFFF1
	help
	  0xFFF1 is CHIP's test vendor. A real product needs an allocated one;
	  a phone will commission a test VID but marks the result uncertified.

config TINYMATTER_PRODUCT_ID
	hex "Matter product ID"
	default 0x8001

config TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_VERIFIER
	string "SPAKE2+ verifier, hex: w0 (32 B) then L (65 B)"
	default "ca92f10ad437760cc8948aefe2d09f142bc1a363824a0079a63e6df097575bb5043609715ad65a53cd3c18a0f80011bf09dbbfbd9453c2b00eed286f5275ecaae60a2e896837995232334c319994d790ee2d09fe5cb4a229503234f8479eb3d308"
	help
	  What the device stores INSTEAD of the setup passcode. Someone who reads
	  this out of flash can verify a commissioner that knows the passcode and
	  cannot impersonate one, which is the point of SPAKE2+'s augmented form.

	  Generate with scripts/spake2p_verifier.py --passcode <p>, which prints
	  this and the two options below. It must be derived from the
	  same passcode, salt and iteration count as the two options below --
	  nothing on the device can check that it was, and a mismatched set fails
	  at Pake3 looking exactly like a wrong passcode.

	  The default was CHIP's own published test verifier for passcode 20202021
	  (TestOnlyCommissionableDataProvider.h:74-79). It is not any more: these
	  values are the source project's own, generated once with the script above. That
	  removes the case where a stranger commissions the lock knowing nothing
	  but the SDK.

	  IT IS STILL ONE PAIRING FOR EVERY BOARD BUILT FROM THIS TREE, and a
	  binary release has to publish its setup code because nothing on the
	  device can print it. Per-board codes need per-board provisioning data,
	  which this build does not have. If the lock guards anything, generate
	  your own with the script and rebuild.

config TINYMATTER_SETUP_PASSCODE
	int "Setup passcode the verifier above was derived from"
	default 58490394
	range 1 134217727
	help
	  The number a user would type into Apple Home. No C file in the module
	  reads it, so the passcode stays out of a module-only image exactly as
	  the augmented SPAKE2+ form intends.

	  It is here because the setup code cannot be recovered from the
	  verifier, and a build that cannot say how to commission itself is a
	  build nobody can use. samples/door-lock runs
	  scripts/spake2p_verifier.py --from-config at build time: it re-derives
	  the verifier from this passcode, refuses to continue if the two
	  disagree (the drift check the device itself cannot do), and compiles
	  the 11-digit manual code into the sample's boot log. A sample image
	  therefore carries the code.

	  Change it and CONFIG_TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_VERIFIER together, both from
	  one run of scripts/spake2p_verifier.py --passcode <p>.

config TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_SALT
	string "SPAKE2+ salt, hex (16 to 32 bytes)"
	default "04f7398d5b49b5b5db8a5e61c8aece1d1a230e3b9085199d854ef034fd30a89b"
	help
	  Sent to the commissioner in PBKDFParamResponse so it can repeat the
	  derivation. It was CHIP's test salt, the ASCII "SPAKE2P Key Salt"; it is
	  now 32 random bytes generated with the passcode above.

config TINYMATTER_SPAKE2P_ITERATIONS
	int "SPAKE2+ PBKDF2 iterations"
	default 20000
	range 1000 100000
	help
	  Also sent in PBKDFParamResponse. Costs the COMMISSIONER time, not this
	  device: the verifier is derived off-device, so a high count is paid by
	  the phone once per pairing, in milliseconds.

	  20000 rather than the 1000 floor. It raises the cost of brute-forcing
	  the passcode back out of a verifier read from flash, which matters here
	  because APPROTECT is deliberately unlocked. Be clear about how much it
	  buys, though: anyone who can dump flash can also read settings_storage,
	  where the source project's reader private key sat in the clear, so this hardens the
	  slower of two paths. The lever that closes both is physical security.

config TINYMATTER_BLE_RX_BUF
	int "Inbound Matter message ceiling, bytes"
	default 1024
	help
	  Size of the BTP reassembly area, and therefore the largest message
	  this node will accept. A peer declaring more than this in its Start
	  fragment is refused before a byte is copied, so setting it low costs
	  compatibility, never memory safety.

config TINYMATTER_BLE_WINDOW
	int "Largest BTP receive window we will grant"
	default 4
	range 2 8

config TINYMATTER_BLE_WQ_STACK
	int "Matter work queue stack, bytes"
	default 4096
	help
	  PROVISIONAL. Two stack sizes in this project were guessed low and both
	  faulted on hardware, so size this from CONFIG_THREAD_ANALYZER_AUTO
	  output rather than by estimate.

	  Raised from 2048 when PASE was wired up, because handling Pake1 is by
	  far the deepest thing this queue does: a 128-byte reply buffer, then
	  matter_pase_sm.c's frame holding pA, pB, Z and V (4 x 65 B) and a
	  SPAKE2+ result, then oberon's P-256 scalar multiplication underneath
	  all of it. That is an argument, not a measurement, which is exactly the
	  reasoning the note above says not to trust -- so measure it and trim.

config TINYMATTER_BLE_WQ_PRIO
	int "Matter work queue thread priority"
	default 10
	help
	  In the source project this had to stay BELOW the UWB ranging work: its
	  Stage 0 measured a CASE handshake at 262.7 ms of solid CPU against a
	  0.66 ms reply margin, and ranging survived only because the load sat at
	  a lower priority.

module = TINYMATTER_BLE
module-str = matter_ble
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

# THREAD logs separately from matter_ble. matter_thread_port.c
# and matter_dns_port.c narrate SRP registration and DNS-SD resolution, which is
# how "the bound lock did not resolve" gets diagnosed; matter_ble carries
# commissioning and fabric handling. Sharing one symbol meant neither could be
# turned down or up without the other.
module = TINYMATTER_THREAD
module-str = matter_thread
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

endif # TINYMATTER_BLE

config TINYMATTER_SRP_DIAG
	bool "Report the SRP client's state every time network data changes"
	# OPENTHREAD, not NET_L2_OPENTHREAD: this reads otSrpClient* state out of
	# matter_thread_port.c and touches no net_if, so it survived the move to
	# standalone OpenThread. It has to say so explicitly: a `depends on` that
	# can no longer be satisfied turns a request for SRP diagnostics into a
	# build with none and no error to say why.
	depends on OPENTHREAD
	default n
	help
	  Diagnosis only, never shipped. Answers one question the existing logs
	  cannot: whether otSrpClientEnableAutoStartMode() ever FOUND a server.

	  A registration that is never sent looks exactly like one that was sent
	  and refused -- both leave the host and services sitting at ToAdd and
	  neither reaches srp_cb(), so the only "SRP failed" line this firmware
	  can print is one that never fires. Measured on hardware 2026-08-03:
	  three minutes as an attached child with a working OMR address, both
	  services queued at ToAdd, and OpenThread's own INFO log carrying no
	  server selection at all.

	  Auto-start picks its server out of Thread network data, so network
	  data changing is the only moment the answer can change. Prints
	  otSrpClientIsRunning(), the chosen server address and the host state
	  on each OT_CHANGED_THREAD_NETDATA. Running means the fault is after
	  server selection; not running once network data has settled means this
	  node is never offered a server -- see ports/zephyr/thread/matter_thread_port.c.

config TINYMATTER_THREAD_DATASET_DUMP
	bool "Print the active Thread operational dataset at attach (BENCH ONLY)"
	depends on OPENTHREAD
	default n
	help
	  Logs the active operational dataset as hex in the raw TLV form
	  `chip-tool pairing ble-thread` takes, when the commissioning window
	  opens -- a button press, not every attach. Tying it to a gesture the
	  owner just made is deliberate.

	  THIS PRINTS THE THREAD NETWORK KEY IN THE CLEAR. Whoever reads that log
	  can join the network. It exists for one bench job: commissioning a second
	  Matter administrator over BLE, which needs a dataset argument, and the
	  only safe dataset to pass is the one the node is already on -- anything
	  else moves the node to a different network and takes it out of its home.

	  Default n and it must stay that way in anything that ships. There is no
	  runtime gate: a build either discloses the key on every attach or cannot.

config TINYMATTER_THREAD
	bool "Thread transport: attach, SRP registration and operational UDP"
	depends on TINYMATTER_BLE && OPENTHREAD
	default n
	help
	  Compile ports/zephyr/thread/: the node joins the Thread network a
	  commissioner hands it, registers its operational service over SRP and
	  carries Matter over UDP on port 5540.

	  Off, the Thread seams resolve to ports/zephyr/matter_thread_stub.c,
	  which answers as a node with no network: the stack cannot be started
	  and the node never attaches, so a commissioner that sends a Thread
	  dataset is told so.

config TINYMATTER_CLEAR_ON_BOOT
	bool "Erase the stored Matter fabrics at boot"
	depends on TINYMATTER_BLE
	default n
	help
	  Atomically advances the `mf2` identity epoch before it is read, so the
	  board comes up with no fabric and advertises commissionable.

	  Preferred over `west flash --erase` because it clears everything a
	  controller can see while leaving OpenThread's own settings alone, so
	  the board rejoins its Thread network instead of being commissioned
	  onto one.

	  It erases on EVERY boot. Flash it once, then flash an image without it.
